{Hamilton (Oxford) defended with the Petroff, and the game proceeded regularly
for 19 moves, when Allen captured a pawn, which the Oxonian left en prise...
position after 28 Qe2:} 28... Kh7 29. Ne5 (29. Qxc4 Rxf3 30. gxf3 Qxh3+) 29...
Bxe5 30. dxe5 Nxe5 31. Be3 Rf3 32. Kh2 Qd6 33. g3 Qc6 34. Rd1 Rxf2+ 35. Qxf2
Nf3+ 36. Kg2 Nd4+ 37. Kf1 Qh1+ 38. Qg1 Qf3+ 39. Bf2 Qxd1+ 40. Kg2 Qf3+ 41. Kh2
Ne2 42. Qg2 Qxg2+ 43. Kxg2 Nc1 44. Bxa7 Nd3 45. b4 Ne1+ 46. Kf1 Nf3 47. h4 Nh2+
{and three moves later, time being called, Mr. Hoffer adjudicated the game as
drawn. The spectators were deeply interested all through this spirited game.
Sources: Chess Monthly, Vol. 10, p238; The Field, 6 April 1889, p480 (but The
Field gives Hamilton as White - this is surely wrong)} 1/2-1/2

